steps.body:
Four feed handlers, one per exchange, push ticks into one queue, and four strategy threads each take whichever tick comes next. The `SpscQueue` from *A Lock-Free SPSC Ring Buffer* can't serve them: two producers would load the same `tail_` and write the same slot.

The simplest **MPMC** (multi-producer, multi-consumer) queue is that ring with a mutex around every call, as the `TickQueue` of *Threads, Data Races, and Mutexes* locked its `std::queue`:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

{{code}}

int main() {
    LockedQueue<int, 4> q;
    std::thread a([&q] { q.try_push(100); });
    std::thread b([&q] { q.try_push(250); });
    a.join();
    b.join();
    int x = 0, y = 0;
    q.try_pop(x);
    q.try_pop(y);
    std::cout << x + y << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
template <typename T, std::size_t N>
class LockedQueue {
public:
    bool try_push(const T& v) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m_);
        if (tail_ - head_ == N) return false;  // full
        slots_[tail_++ & (N - 1)] = v;
        return true;
    }
    bool try_pop(T& out) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m_);
        if (head_ == tail_) return false;  // empty
        out = slots_[head_++ & (N - 1)];
        return true;
    }

private:
    std::mutex m_;
    std::array<T, N> slots_{};
    std::size_t head_ = 0, tail_ = 0;
};
```

A lock-free one, in the style of Dmitry Vyukov's bounded queue, changes two things. A thread claims a position with a **CAS** (compare-and-swap), the `compare_exchange_weak` of *Atomics and Memory Ordering*, on `tail_` or `head_`, so no two threads get the same one. And since a claimed slot isn't written yet, each slot keeps a `turn`. Each trip around the ring's `N` slots is a **lap**, and on lap `L`, turn `2L` means the slot waits for that lap's push, and `2L + 1` means it's full and waits for the pop. A push at position `pos`, the `tail_` it loaded, is on lap `pos / N`. It reads the slot's turn with acquire:

- Below its own: last lap's value is still there, so the queue is full.
- Equal: it tries the CAS. Winning makes the slot its own, so it writes the value and stores the next turn with release, which publishes the value as the SPSC queue's `tail_` store did.
- Above: another push took the slot, so it reloads `tail_`.

A pop mirrors it on `head_`, copies the value out, and stores turn `2L + 2` with release. That hands the slot to the next lap's push, whose acquire load of the turn makes it write only after the copy. The CAS can be relaxed, since it only hands out positions, and the values travel through the turns.

Lock-free doesn't mean faster at everything. Built with `// -O2`, 4 producers and 4 consumers moving 10^6 numbers flat out took about 150 ms here through this queue and 100 ms through the lock: threads that lose a CAS retry at once and keep the cache lines moving, while a mutex puts the losers to sleep. At the pace of a real feed, 2 feeds sending a tick every 3 µs to 2 strategies, the slowest hand-off in 100 took about 0.6 µs through this queue and 11 µs through the lock, where a strategy that found the lock taken slept in the kernel until woken. The times are rough and emulated, and this Mac showed the same pattern. Many hot paths skip MPMC altogether, with one `SpscQueue` per feed that the strategy polls in turn.

