A risk thread publishes new limits several times a second, and every gateway checks each order against the current ones. The risk thread builds a new `Limits`, swaps the shared pointer to it with `exchange`, which stores a value and returns the old one in one atomic step, and is left holding the old one. When can it `delete` that? A gateway may have loaded the old pointer an instant before the swap and still be reading through it. Delete too soon and that read is a use after free. Never delete and memory grows without end. C++ code uses one of two schemes for **safe memory reclamation**.

**Hazard pointers.** Each reader owns a slot that announces the pointer it's using. It loads the pointer, stores it in its slot, then loads the pointer again. If it's still the same, the writer can't have retired it before the announcement. The writer deletes a retired pointer only once no slot holds it:

```cpp harness
#include <atomic>
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>

struct Limits {
    int64_t version;
    int64_t max_qty;
};

std::atomic<Limits*> current{new Limits{7, 700}};
std::atomic<Limits*> hazard[3];  // one slot per reader, all nullptr to start

{{code}}

int main() {
    const Limits* l = protect(0);
    std::cout << l->version << ' ' << l->max_qty << '\n';
    hazard[0].store(nullptr);  // done with it
    delete current.load();
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
const Limits* protect(int reader) {
    Limits* p = current.load();
    for (;;) {
        hazard[reader].store(p);         // announce it
        Limits* again = current.load();  // still current, so not retired before the announcement
        if (again == p) return p;
        p = again;
    }
}
```

That keeps the waiting garbage small, at the cost of a store and a second load for every pointer a reader follows.

**Epochs.** A global counter, the **epoch**, moves forward. A reader announces the current epoch when it starts reading and goes idle when it's done, once per batch of reads instead of once per pointer. The writer tags each retired object with the epoch, and moves the epoch on only once every reader that isn't idle has announced the current one. An object retired in epoch `e` can be deleted once the epoch reaches `e + 2`: by then every reader that could have seen it has gone idle. Reads are cheap, but one stalled reader holds up every delete. The Linux kernel's RCU, read-copy-update, is built on the same idea. C++26 adds both schemes, as `std::hazard_pointer` and `std::rcu_domain`, and the Folly library has them today.

Both need seq_cst. A reader's store to its slot must be visible before its next load of the shared pointer: a store followed by a load, the one order that release and acquire don't give. *Atomics and Memory Ordering* showed it, with two threads that each stored a flag with release, loaded the other's with acquire, and both read `false`.

