steps.body:
A feed handler updates the top of the book a million times a second, and a dozen strategies poll it. A queue would hand every update to every reader, when each wants only the latest. A mutex would let the readers hold up the writer, and the writer must never wait. A **seqlock** gives readers the latest value and never blocks the writer. The writer bumps a sequence number to odd, writes the fields, and bumps it to even again. A reader copies the fields between two loads of the number, and copies again if the loads differ or the first was odd, since then a write overlapped its copy. Readers never write shared memory and never take a lock, so the writer never waits for them. The reader pays instead: it may copy more than once, and a writer that never pauses can keep it retrying.

Two details make it correct C++. The fields are `std::atomic`s read and written relaxed, because a reader copies while the writer writes, and plain fields would be a data race even though the torn copy gets thrown away. And `std::atomic_thread_fence`, a **fence**, orders those relaxed accesses against the sequence number without being an access itself: a release fence after the odd store keeps the field writes from moving above it, and an acquire fence before the second load keeps the field reads from moving below it. On x86-64 all of it is plain `mov`s, and the fences vanish. g++ 10.2 in the sandbox makes this reader of two fields:

```text
read_quote:
.L3:
        mov     rdx, QWORD PTR seq[rip]
        mov     r9, QWORD PTR bid[rip]
        mov     r8, QWORD PTR ask[rip]
        mov     rax, QWORD PTR seq[rip]
        cmp     rax, rdx
        jne     .L3
        test    al, 1
        jne     .L3
        mov     rax, r9
        mov     rdx, r8
        ret
```

Apple clang on the Mac makes the first load an `ldapr` and the acquire fence a `dmb ishld` barrier (ARM64).

### C++20's waiting tools

g++ 10.2 has no `<latch>`, `<barrier>`, or `<semaphore>` (`fatal error: latch: No such file or directory`), so this is Apple clang 21 on a Mac, `clang++ -std=c++20`. g++ has them from version 11, and MSVC with `/std:c++20`. A `std::latch` counts down once and lets every waiter through at 0, here to wait until each symbol thread has loaded its history and then to start them together. A `std::barrier` is reusable: each day every thread arrives and waits, and its completion function runs once, on one thread, before any of them goes on. A `std::counting_semaphore<2>` lets at most 2 threads past `acquire()` at a time, like a venue that allows 2 sessions:

```cpp norun
#include <atomic>
#include <barrier>
#include <iostream>
#include <latch>
#include <semaphore>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    const long pnl[3][3] = {{120, -40, 75}, {-30, 60, 10}, {45, 15, -90}};  // [symbol][day]
    long today[3] = {};  // each symbol's thread writes only its own slot
    int day = 0;
    std::latch loaded(3), start(1);                  // count down once, then let every waiter through
    std::barrier end_of_day(3, [&]() noexcept {      // runs once a day, after all 3 arrive
        std::cout << "day " << ++day << ": " << today[0] + today[1] + today[2] << '\n';
    });
    std::counting_semaphore<2> sessions(2);          // at most 2 threads at the venue at once
    std::atomic<int> at_venue{0}, too_many{0};

    std::vector<std::thread> threads;
    for (int s = 0; s < 3; ++s) {
        threads.emplace_back([&, s] {
            loaded.count_down();  // this symbol's history is loaded
            start.wait();
            for (int d = 0; d < 3; ++d) {
                today[s] = pnl[s][d];
                sessions.acquire();  // waits while 2 others hold a session
                if (at_venue.fetch_add(1) + 1 > 2) too_many.fetch_add(1);
                at_venue.fetch_sub(1);
                sessions.release();
                end_of_day.arrive_and_wait();  // nobody starts day d + 1 before all finish day d
            }
        });
    }
    loaded.wait();
    std::cout << "loaded 3 symbols\n";
    start.count_down();
    for (std::thread& t : threads) t.join();
    std::cout << "more than 2 at the venue: " << too_many << " times\n";
}
```

```text
loaded 3 symbols
day 1: 135
day 2: 35
day 3: -5
more than 2 at the venue: 0 times
```

C++20 also gives every atomic `wait`, `notify_one`, and `notify_all`, which g++ 10.2 lacks too.

